  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite and radar imagery show a \nbroad upper-level trough over the western United States with \nlower amplitude shortwave energy moving across the Dakotas and \nMinnesota. At the surface, a warm front extends from northern Iowa\nto central Illinois, with low pressure organizing over Iowa. \nA potent 30 to 35 knot low-level jet riding over the warm front \nis supporting a line of deep thunderstorms from southeast \nMinnesota into southern Wisconsin (south of Wood and Manitowoc \ncounties) where localized rainfall rates exceed 1 inch per hour. \nBecause the main convective core remains south of the area, the \nrisk for flooding across central and east-central Wisconsin has \nended. Over northern Wisconsin, high pressure centered over \nnorthern Ontario and Quebec continues to feed cooler, drier air \nsouthward. This has kept areas north of Highway 10 mostly dry \nearly this morning.\n\nPeriods of rain continue through tonight: \nAs the shortwave energy and associated surface low move east\nacross the area today, rain is expected to gradually increase in\ncoverage from west to east. Broad upper-level ascent in the right\nrear quadrant of an upper jet streak combined with low-level warm\nadvection will generate efficient rainfall production, supported\nby precipitable water values in the 90th to 95th percentile.\nAdditional rainfall amounts of 0.25 to 1.00 inch are expected \ngenerally south of Highway 29 through this evening before the low \nexits to the east overnight and brings precipitation to an end.\n\nQuiet weather returns for next week:\nFor the long term period from Sunday through Friday, a prolonged\nstretch of quiet and dry weather is anticipated across north-\ncentral and northeast Wisconsin. Shortwave energy originating from\nthe western trough will slowly migrate across the central Plains\nand southern Great Lakes toward the East Coast during the week,\nkeeping deep moisture and rain chances suppressed well south of\nthe local area. Strong high pressure centered over northern\nOntario and Quebec will maintain an expansive plume of dry air\nacross northern Wisconsin, keeping rain probabilities below 10\npercent through Friday. Under clear to partly cloudy skies,\nefficient radiational cooling on Monday and Tuesday nights will \ndrop overnight low temperatures into the mid to upper 30s across \nnorth-central Wisconsin, leading to patchy frost formation in low-\nlying areas. A steady warming trend will take place throughout \nthe week as afternoon high temperatures rise from the low and mid \n60s on Monday and Tuesday into the low and mid 70s by Friday.",
